  SAFETY_OVERRIDES_GENERIC_GENERIC_CV_9.

Do NOT infer the function or causal effect of a named artifact merely from its name.

Determine what each field objectively establishes and what it does not.

Preserve the original hash and distinguish:
- raw evidence;
- extracted transcript;
- derived chronology;
- interpretation.

PRIMARY ADA QUESTIONS

A. COVERAGE

Research whether and under what theory Apple/Siri/iOS services may fall within ADA Title III.

Analyze:
- 42 U.S.C. § 12182;
- definition of public accommodation;
- digital-only service theories;
- nexus-to-physical-place theories;
- Apple retail stores and other physical public accommodations;
- services, privileges, advantages, and goods associated with those places;
- the significance, if any, of a paid Apple Developer relationship.

Research controlling law for every realistically relevant forum, especially:
- Fifth Circuit;
- Ninth Circuit;
- Supreme Court;
and identify conflicts with other circuits where material.

Include:
- Robles v. Domino’s Pizza;
- Magee v. Coca-Cola Refreshments;
- other leading website/app/public-accommodation cases;
- current DOJ position on digital accessibility.

Do not assume Title III coverage. Resolve it as far as present law permits.

B. ELIGIBILITY / SCREENING CRITERIA

Analyze:
42 U.S.C. § 12182(b)(2)(A)(i)
and 28 C.F.R. § 36.301.

Question:
Could an automated behavioral/safety classifier that uses linguistic characteristics correlated with a disability constitute an eligibility criterion or practice that screens out or tends to screen out disabled users?

Determine what proof of causation, disparate treatment, disparate impact, knowledge, or nexus is required.

C. REASONABLE MODIFICATION

Analyze:
42 U.S.C. § 12182(b)(2)(A)(ii)
and 28 C.F.R. § 36.302.

Research:
- what counts as a policy, practice, or procedure when implemented in software;
- individualized modification duties;
- PGA Tour, Inc. v. Martin;
- necessity;
- reasonableness;
- fundamental alteration;
- technological feasibility;
- cost/administrative burden where relevant.

Do NOT assume automated safety filters are inherently unmodifiable.

Evaluate possible modifications such as:
- disability/accessibility communication profile;
- classifier bypass for routine device functions;
- profanity-neutral processing;
- requirement for objective risk indicators beyond linguistic intensity;
- human review/escalation;
- user override;
- alternate execution path;
- safety response that does not disable unrelated device functions;
- opt-out from behavioral inference where technically feasible.

For each, determine whether it is:
- legally plausible;
- technically plausible;
- factually unsupported;
- potentially a fundamental alteration.

D. EFFECTIVE COMMUNICATION / AUXILIARY AIDS

Analyze:
42 U.S.C. § 12182(b)(2)(A)(iii)
and 28 C.F.R. § 36.303.

Determine:
- whether cognitive, speech, neurological, or communication disabilities can implicate these provisions;
- whether a voice assistant can itself function as an accessibility interface;
- whether degradation of that interface can constitute denial of effective communication;
- limits of this theory;
- whether alternative means must be offered when a preferred method would be an undue burden or fundamental alteration.

Distinguish direct authority from analogy.

E. SAFETY / DIRECT THREAT

Analyze:
28 C.F.R. §§ 36.208 and 36.301(b)
and relevant Supreme Court authority.

Determine:
- requirements for individualized assessment;
- actual risk versus speculation/stereotype/generalization;
- best available objective evidence;
- probability/severity analysis;
- obligation to consider risk-mitigating modifications.

Compare those requirements to the factual behavior reflected in the Siri logs without assuming what internal classifier reasoning occurred.

F. DISABILITY / REQUEST / NOTICE

Research:
- what must be shown regarding disability;
- whether a formal accommodation request is required;
- what constitutes adequate notice that a modification is necessary;
- whether repeated statements explaining that a communication pattern is disability-related matter legally;
- how the analysis changes if the system cannot itself process accommodation requests.

G. ENFORCEMENT AND REMEDIES

Research:
- DOJ Civil Rights Division jurisdiction;
- Title III complaint procedure;
- DOJ mediation;
- pattern-or-practice and general-public-importance authority;
- private injunctive relief;
- attorneys’ fees;
- limits on private monetary damages;
- damages available when DOJ brings an enforcement action;
- civil penalties;
- preservation obligations once a dispute is reasonably foreseeable.

Do not exaggerate available remedies.

SECONDARY LEGAL LANES

Research separately and do not contaminate the ADA analysis with them.

1. FDA / SOFTWARE AS A MEDICAL DEVICE
Determine whether behavioral/crisis classification by a general consumer assistant could fall within FDA device jurisdiction.

Focus heavily on:
- statutory definition of device;
- intended use;
- FDA digital-health guidance;
- Clinical Decision Support exclusions;
- general wellness exclusions;
- whether a safety classifier constitutes diagnosis, treatment, mitigation, or prevention.

Treat “SaMD violation” as UNRESOLVED unless primary law actually supports it.

2. FCC / CVAA
Determine whether Siri or the relevant functionality qualifies as:
- advanced communications service;
- equipment/software used for ACS;
- an accessibility feature subject to Section 716 or related rules.

Identify exact jurisdictional limits.

3. FTC
Research whether representations concerning:
- accessibility;
